Q: HttpIOException: The response ended prematurely A: <p>The most likely cause is <strong>HTTP/2 protocol incompatibility</strong> with your Java server. <code>HttpClient</code> in .NET 8 uses HTTP/2 by default, which has stricter requirements than HTTP/1.1 that .NET Framework's <code>HttpWebRequest</code> used.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>
services.AddHttpClient(HttpClientConstants.CoreHttpClient)
    .ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler(() =&gt; new HttpClientHandler
    {
        AutomaticDecompression = DecompressionMethods.GZip | DecompressionMethods.Deflate,
        UseCookies = false,
        AllowAutoRedirect = false,
        UseDefaultCredentials = true
    })
    .ConfigureHttpClient(client =&gt;
    {
        // Force HTTP/1.1
        client.DefaultRequestVersion = HttpVersion.Version11;
    });
</code></pre>
<p>This matches the behavior your .NET Framework code had and often resolves the &quot;response ended prematurely&quot; error with servers that don't fully implement HTTP/2 correctly.</p>

Q: std::call_once, when should it be used? A: <p>Caching and lazy evaluation. Suppose an immutable class has a cheap-to-store but expensive-to-compute property, <code>double foo() const;</code>. Rather than compute it on demand or compute it up front, you could do</p>
<pre><code>private:
    mutable std::once_flag m_flag;
    mutable double m_foo;
    double doCalcFoo() const; // Expensive!
public:
    double foo() const {
        std::call_once(m_flag, [this] { m_foo = doCalcFoo(); });
        return m_foo;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>While you could do</p>
<pre><code>private:
    mutable std::optional&lt;double&gt; m_foo;
    mutable std::mutex m_fooMutex;
    double doCalcFoo() const; // Expensive!
public:
    double foo() const {
        std::lock_guard lock{m_fooMutex};
        if (!m_foo) {
            m_foo = doCalcFoo();
        }
        return *m_foo;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>that's more bytes (40 + 16 = 56 bytes versus 4 + 8 + padding = 16 on Clang), less performance-optimal, and violates Parent's Better-Code goal of &quot;No Raw Synchronization Primitives&quot;: (<a href="https://sean-parent.stlab.cc/presentations/2016-08-08-concurrency/2016-08-08-concurrency.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://sean-parent.stlab.cc/presentations/2016-08-08-concurrency/2016-08-08-concurrency.pdf</a> slide 6 through 11).</p>
<h2>Non Usecase</h2>
<p>I've seen this use:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int thisIsNotTheRightWay() {
    static std::once_flag once; //&lt; This magic static has a built-in call-once.
    static int result; //&lt; So does this!
    std::call_once([&amp;] { result = expensive(); });
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p>which is correct behavior, but overly complex: instead that can be</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int cachedExpensiveCall() {
    int const result = expensive();
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
